"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunklearnalgorithms=globalThis.webpackChunklearnalgorithms||[]).push([[8982],{3755(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"tag":{"label":"Parallelism","permalink":"/concepts/tags/parallelism","allTagsPath":"/concepts/tags","count":5,"items":[{"id":"advanced-execution-models/parallel-algorithms","title":"Concurrency and Parallelism","description":"Until now, every algorithm in this course has been sequential: one instruction after another, on a single processor. But the computers we use, from phones to servers, have several processor cores, and a sequential program uses only one of them. Parallel algorithms divide the work so that several cores can do it at the same time.","permalink":"/concepts/advanced-execution-models/parallel-algorithms"},{"id":"advanced-execution-models/exercises","title":"Exercises: Parallel Algorithms","description":"These exercises review the three lessons of the chapter the solutions are grouped at the end of the page.","permalink":"/concepts/advanced-execution-models/exercises"},{"id":"advanced-execution-models/parallel-performance","title":"Performance of Parallel Algorithms","description":"A parallel program is only worth its extra complexity if it is faster. But how much faster can it be? Does doubling the number of cores halve the time? This lesson presents the tools to answer these questions: how the complexity of an algorithm relates to its parallel execution time, how to measure the benefit of parallelism, and Amdahl\'s law, which sets a hard limit on it.","permalink":"/concepts/advanced-execution-models/parallel-performance"},{"id":"advanced-execution-models/proofs","title":"Proofs: Parallel Algorithms","description":"This page derives the formulas of the performance lesson and proves that the Fork/Join scheme computes the same result as the sequential algorithm. Times are measured relative to the sequential time, which we take as 1 unless stated otherwise.","permalink":"/concepts/advanced-execution-models/proofs"},{"id":"advanced-execution-models/fork-join-framework","title":"The Fork/Join Framework","description":"Creating and coordinating threads by hand is laborious and error-prone. Since Java 7, the package java.util.concurrent includes the Fork/Join framework, designed precisely to execute divide and conquer algorithms in parallel, using all the available processors automatically. This lesson explains how it works and how to use it, with the examples of the course repository.","permalink":"/concepts/advanced-execution-models/fork-join-framework"}],"unlisted":false}}')}}]);